.TH TNT-CHAT 7 "2026-09-08" "TNT 1.3.1"
.SH NAME
tnt-chat \- use the TNT interactive terminal interface
.SH DESCRIPTION
The TNT interactive interface is a shared chat room presented through an
SSH terminal session.
By default it has no modes: typing types, and commands begin with
.BR / .
The
.B vim
keymap restores the modal interface described under
.BR "VIM KEYMAP" .
.P
Public messages are visible to the room and are persisted by the server.
Private messages are kept only in the participating sessions.
.SH CONNECTING
Connect with a terminal and request a PTY:
.P
.EX
ssh \-p 2222 any-user@chat.example.com
.EE
.P
The SSH login name controls authentication.
It does not select the interactive
nickname.
TNT prompts for a nickname after login.
An empty or invalid nickname becomes
.BR anonymous .
Nicknames are limited to 20 Unicode characters.
They must be unique among active
sessions without regard to ASCII letter case.
The names
.BR * ,
.BR system ,
.BR 系统 ,
and the
.B module:
prefix are reserved for internal messages.
.P
If the administrator configured an access token,
enter it as the SSH password.
The token is shared and does not authenticate the nickname.
.SH KEYS
Typing inserts at the caret.
Nothing a user types moves the session into a different keyboard state.
.TP
.B Enter
Send the current nonempty message, or run a command when the line begins
with a recognised
.BR / word.
.TP
.B Ctrl-J, Alt-Enter
Insert a line break without sending.
.B Ctrl-J
is a literal line feed and works on every terminal;
.B Alt-Enter
depends on the terminal sending
.B Esc
before the carriage return, and is accepted only in this keymap, because
.B Esc
is how the vim keymap reaches NORMAL.
Pasted text keeps the line breaks it arrived with.
.TP
.B Esc
Dismiss the help or a command result.
It never changes what the keyboard does.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Clear the input.
When the input is already empty, TNT shows how to leave.
.TP
.B PgUp, PgDn
Scroll the room history without leaving the input.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move the caret between the lines of the message being composed.
While the input is empty they instead recall this session's last 16 sent
messages, so a message being composed is never replaced.
.SS Input region
The input region is one row tall and grows with the message, to at most six
rows.
Past that it scrolls and keeps the caret in view.
The room history shrinks by the rows the input region takes.
A message may be at most 1023 bytes as stored, and a line break costs two of
them; the counter that appears past 80 percent counts stored bytes.
.SS Commands
A line that begins with
.B /
and a recognised command word runs that command; the leading
.B /
is not part of it.
Write
.B //
to send a message that really begins with a slash.
Anything else, including a path such as
.IR /usr/local/bin ,
is sent as an ordinary message.
.SH VIM KEYMAP
The vim keymap is the modal interface: INSERT, NORMAL, and COMMAND, with
.B :
commands.
It is unchanged.
Enable it in any of three ways:
.TP
.B /vim
Switch this session.
.B /vim off
returns to the default keys.
.TP
.BI "ssh vim@" host
The SSH login name selects neither identity nor nickname, so it is free to
carry this request.
Put it in
.BR ssh_config (5)
once.
.TP
.B \-\-keymap vim
Make vim the default for a whole server.
See
.BR tnt (8).
.SS INSERT mode
INSERT mode edits and sends public messages.
.TP
.B Enter
Send the current nonempty message.
.TP
.B Ctrl-J
Insert a line break without sending.
.B Alt-Enter
is not accepted here, because
.B Esc
enters NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B Esc
Enter NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B Left, Right
Move the caret one character.
Text typed next is inserted at the caret.
.TP
.B Home, End
Move the caret to the start or the end of the display line it is on.
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the character before the caret.
.TP
.B Delete
Delete the character under the caret.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the word before the caret.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Delete the input line.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move through this session's last 16 sent messages.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete an online nickname in the trailing
.B @
token.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Enter NORMAL mode.
.P
A caret movement never splits a character, an emoji, or a flag.
An escape sequence this build does not recognise is ignored; it does not
change modes.
.P
Bracketed multiline paste is inserted at the caret as one message, keeping
its line breaks.
A carriage return, alone or before a line feed, becomes one line break; a tab
becomes a space.
Input that exceeds the message limit or contains invalid UTF-8 is rejected
with a terminal bell.
.SS NORMAL mode
NORMAL mode browses the in-memory room history.
The view follows new messages until the user scrolls upward.
.TP
.B i, a, A, o, O
Enter INSERT mode.
.TP
.B :
Enter COMMAND mode.
.TP
.B /
Enter COMMAND mode with
.B search
preloaded.
.TP
.B j, Down
Scroll one line toward newer messages.
.TP
.B k, Up
Scroll one line toward older messages.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D, Ctrl-U
Scroll half a page down or up.
.TP
.B Ctrl-F, Ctrl-B
Scroll a page down or up.
.TP
.B PageDown, PageUp
Scroll a page down or up.
.TP
.B End, Home
Jump to the newest or oldest visible message.
.TP
.B g, G
Jump to the oldest or newest visible message.
.TP
.B ?
Open the interactive key reference.
.TP
.B Ctrl-C
Disconnect.
.SS COMMAND mode
COMMAND mode edits a command without the leading colon shown in command
documentation.
.TP
.B Enter
Execute the command.
.TP
.B Esc
Cancel the command and enter NORMAL mode.
.TP
.B Backspace
Delete the preceding Unicode character.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the preceding word.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Delete the command line.
.TP
.B Up, Down
Move through this session's last 16 commands.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete a command name.
For
.BR msg ,
.BR theme ,
and
.BR lang ,
it also completes the first argument.
.SH MESSAGES
A public message must be valid UTF-8 and may contain at most 1023 bytes as
stored, where a line break costs two.
A line break is the only C0 or C1 control character accepted as message text;
every other one, and DEL, is refused.
.TP
.BI "/me " action
Send an action message.
The stored action includes the nickname.
It must fit the same message limit.
.TP
.BI @ nickname
Mention an online user in a public message.
The recipient receives a terminal bell and highlight.
.P
A message that is wider than the terminal wraps onto as many rows as it
needs, and the continuation rows align under the first row's text.
Wrapping never splits a character, an emoji, or a flag.
.P
The room keeps the 100 most recent public messages in memory.
Use
.B :last
or
.B :search
to read valid records from the active persistent log.
.SH COMMANDS
Commands are introduced by
.B :
in NORMAL mode.
The following aliases have identical behavior.
.TP
.B :users, :list, :who
List active nicknames and connection durations.
.TP
.BI ":msg " "user message"
.TQ
.BI ":w " "user message"
Send a private message to an active user.
Both the sender and recipient receive a session-local inbox copy.
.TP
.BI ":reply " message
.TQ
.BI ":r " message
Reply to the most recent private-message peer.
.TP
.B :inbox
Open the private-message inbox, newest first.
Unread incoming messages are marked and counted until displayed.
The open inbox refreshes when a new private message arrives.
.TP
.B :inbox clear
Clear the inbox and the current reply target.
.TP
.BI ":nick " name
.TQ
.BI ":name " name
Change the current nickname.
.TP
.BI ":last " [count]
Display up to
.I count
valid messages from the active log's recent record window.
The range is 1 through 50; the default is 10.
.TP
.BI ":search " keyword
Search persisted usernames and content without regard to ASCII letter case.
The last 15 matches are displayed.
.TP
.B :mute-joins, :mute
Toggle display of join and leave notices for this session.
.TP
.BI ":theme " [name]
.TQ
.BI ":color " [name]
Show or set the session colour theme.
Valid names are
.BR cyan ,
.BR green ,
.BR magenta ,
.BR blue ,
.BR amber ,
.BR red ,
and
.BR mono .
The default is
.BR cyan .
.TP
.BI ":lang " [en|zh]
.TQ
.BI ":language " [en|zh]
Show or set the session interface language.
.TP
.B :help
Open the concise interactive manual.
.TP
.B :clear, :cls
Clear the current command output.
.TP
.B :q, :quit, :exit
Disconnect.
.SH PAGERS
Command output and help use a pager.
.TP
.B q, Esc
Close the pager.
.TP
.B j, k, Down, Up
Scroll one line down or up.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D, Ctrl-U
Scroll half a page down or up.
.TP
.B Ctrl-F, Space
Scroll a page down.
.TP
.B Ctrl-B, b
Scroll a page up.
.TP
.B PageDown, PageUp
Scroll a page down or up.
.TP
.B End, Home
Jump to the end or beginning.
.TP
.B g, G
Jump to the beginning or end.
.TP
.B r, R
Refresh live inbox output.
These keys have no effect in other command-output pages.
.TP
.B l, L
Cycle the interface language while the help page is open.
.SH CAVEATS
The private inbox holds at most 16 messages per session.
Older messages are discarded when it fills.
Private messages are not written to
.IR messages.log ,
do not appear in public history or exec output, and are lost at disconnect.
.P
Public nicknames are labels, not authenticated identities.
Another session may reuse a nickname after its owner disconnects.
.P
The terminal must support the ANSI control sequences used by the interface.
.SH EXAMPLES
Send an action from INSERT mode:
.P
.EX
/me waves
.EE
.P
Send and then answer a private message from COMMAND mode:
.P
.EX
:msg alice hello
:reply thanks
.EE
.P
Search persistent history:
.P
.EX
:search release
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR tntctl (1),
.BR tnt\-message\-log (5),
.BR tnt\-exec (7),
.BR tnt (8)
